{"id": "b_tension_fault", "query": "Where do my notes disagree about whether problems are the system's fault or my own responsibility?", "key_facts": ["Structural oppression is an invisible foot: entire systems silently advantage some and disadvantage others — not individuals being cruel", "Every compromise we make is a fraud committed against ourselves, happening the moment we enter the contract", "People become fraudsters because the end justifies the means — obsessed with goals, they give up their own fundamental morals", "It is tough to blame yourself, but being critically objective of what hurts ensures we don't repeat the same mistake"], "hard": true}
{"id": "b_lineage_compromise", "query": "How has my view on compromise changed from 2017 to now?", "key_facts": ["We expect we can live with a compromise, but we never can — our innate identity will never leave us", "Breaking a contract is not the fraud; the fraud happens the moment a person enters an unfavourable contract", "My marriage was born out of a compromise with myself — an acceptance that maybe this is the maximum I can feel for someone", "I am not desperate for companionship anymore; I will wait and let myself feel and express true and honest emotions"], "hard": true}
{"id": "b_scale_erosion", "query": "What single pattern shows up in my notes on relationships, career, and society?", "key_facts": ["Any relationship where the two people are not equals — one dictating the terms of the other's life — is toxic", "Modern employment sits on a thin line with slavery — bonds and contracts with little freedom", "Social contracts fail when it becomes impossible to abide by them or the contract works against you", "Survival is the tendency to keep one's form and structure intact against decay from interactions with the surroundings"], "hard": true}
{"id": "b_identity_assembly", "query": "What have I concluded about identity across everything I've written?", "key_facts": ["The only fundamental of life is survival — keeping physical form and structure intact", "The genes of a unicellular organism contain a large part of its identity", "Our innate nature and innate identity never leave us, so a compromise against them must eventually be confronted", "I had built a limiting idea of who I am and what I expected from people; it was time to un-limit myself"], "hard": true}
{"id": "b_meta_recurrence", "query": "Which of my ideas recur at more than one scale?", "key_facts": ["An ASI is much like a living being — building one on the principles of survival forces hard questions", "All systems tend to resist change; elasticity — absorbing shocks and evolving with each — makes a system resilient or anti-fragile", "The toxicity framework spans parent-child, romantic, citizen-government and fan-football-club relationships", "All emotions are score-keeping for survival; love finds a partner who makes survival in a complicated society easier"], "hard": true}
{"id": "b_bridge_resp_identity", "query": "How does my thinking on personal responsibility connect to my thinking on identity preservation?", "key_facts": ["Being critically objective about what hurts is a human tendency to ensure we don't repeat the mistake", "The fraud we do is primarily against ourselves — a compromise our innate identity can never live with", "The system ensures some remain behind even when they play by the rules — invisible weights accumulate", "I have finally stopped missing the Vardan from the summer of 2011 and feel comfortable in my own skin"], "hard": true}
